# Day 01 – DevOps Fundamentals

## Key Questions Covered
- What is DevOps?
- Why DevOps?

---

## What is DevOps?
DevOps is a **culture or practice** adopted by organizations to **improve their ability to deliver applications**.

The **ultimate goal of any organization is delivery**, and DevOps helps reduce the time required to deliver new versions of applications from **days or weeks to hours or minutes**.

DevOps is not only about delivery. It focuses on:
- **Automation** – reducing manual work to improve delivery speed
- **Quality** – ensuring customer expectations are met
- **Monitoring** – tracking application health to maintain quality
- **Testing** – validating automation and ensuring correct functionality

### Revised Definition
DevOps is a **process of improving application delivery** by ensuring:
- Proper automation  
- Maintained quality  
- Continuous monitoring  
- Continuous testing  

The primary goal of a DevOps engineer is to **eliminate manual processes** and **speed up application delivery**.

---

## Why DevOps?
Before DevOps, application delivery involved multiple teams and heavy manual effort.

### Traditional Roles Involved
- **Developer** – wrote application code  
- **System Administrator** – created servers using platforms like VMware or OpenStack  
- **Build & Release Engineer** – deployed applications after code check-in (SVN/CVS)  
- **Tester** – tested the application on servers  
- **Server Administrator** – set up application servers  

This process was **slow, inefficient**, and could take **days to months**.

DevOps emerged to **automate this entire workflow** and improve delivery speed and reliability.

DevOps is a **culture** because it requires a **single team** to adapt new tools and processes together to improve efficiency.


